File: aruhan_system_orchestrator.support
Classification: INTERNAL — TRUSTED ENGINEERS ONLY
Module: ARUHAN System Orchestrator (Y‑Prime Edition)
1. Overview
The System Orchestrator is ARUHAN’s central coordination layer, responsible for:

sequencing all cognitive modules

synchronizing emotional, memory, knowledge, and task states

maintaining global meta‑stability

logging system‑level interactions

ensuring deterministic pipeline execution

It sits above all core modules:

Emotional Engine

Memory Engine

Knowledge Engine

Task/Action Engine

Dialogue Engine

The orchestrator is the “spine” of ARUHAN — nothing runs without it.

2. Pipeline Architecture
The orchestrator executes modules in a strict, deterministic order:

Emotional Engine

Memory Engine

Knowledge Engine

Task/Action Engine

Dialogue Engine

This order is non‑negotiable.

Changing the pipeline order will break:

emotional alignment

memory consistency

knowledge graph stability

task prioritization

conversational coherence

3. Synchronization Layer
The orchestrator computes global meta‑state values:

load — system workload

drift — emotional + cognitive instability

bias — positivity/negativity tilt

sync — alignment between modules

Synchronization uses weighted averages from:

emotional output

memory meta

knowledge meta

task meta

High drift reduces sync.
High sync indicates stable system operation.

4. Logging System
The orchestrator logs:

input message

final output

meta snapshot

Each log entry contains:

Code
{"in": <msg>, "out": <response>, "meta": <meta_state>}
History is capped at 300 entries.

Logs are used for:

debugging

behavioral analysis

drift detection

system audits

5. Meta‑State System
The orchestrator maintains global meta values:

load — queue pressure

drift — cross‑module instability

bias — emotional tilt

sync — alignment score

These values influence:

future task prioritization

emotional modulation

conversational stability

system recovery behavior

6. Module Interfaces
The orchestrator expects each module to expose a .process() method with deterministic output.

Required signatures:

Emotional Engine → process(msg)

Memory Engine → process(msg, emo)

Knowledge Engine → process(msg, mem, emo)

Task Engine → process(msg, emo, mem, know)

Dialogue Engine → process(msg, emo)

Modules must not break these signatures.

7. Rule Pipeline (Execution Order)
The orchestrator executes in this exact order:

emotional processing

memory update

knowledge update

task processing

dialogue generation

synchronization

logging

return final output

This order ensures:

emotional state influences memory

memory influences knowledge

knowledge influences tasks

tasks influence global meta

dialogue reflects all upstream states

8. Debugging Entry Points
Safe to inspect:

meta state

pipeline order

history length

last output

Safe to modify:

history limit

sync formula

load weighting

Do NOT modify:

pipeline order

module call signatures

drift/bias formulas

logging structure

9. Safe Modification Zones
Engineers may safely:

adjust sync formula

adjust load calculation

expand logging metadata

add pipeline hooks (pre/post)

Engineers must NOT:

reorder pipeline

bypass emotional engine

bypass meta‑sync

disable logging

modify module dependencies

10. Expansion Guidelines
When expanding orchestrator capabilities:

maintain deterministic execution

avoid circular dependencies

ensure compatibility with all modules

test under emotional extremes

preserve sync stability

Future integration with the Security AI will add access‑control hooks.

11. Internal Philosophy
The orchestrator is built on:

strict sequencing

deterministic behavior

emotional alignment

modular independence

global stability

It is not meant to be “smart” — it is meant to be reliable.

12. Notes for Trusted Engineers
The orchestrator is the most sensitive module in ARUHAN.

Any modification must be tested across all modules.

Never modify the orchestrator while ARUHAN is running live.

Future versions will include system‑level recovery and watchdog layers.
